'''Duriankari''' (''Duriankere'' or ''Efpan'') is a possibly extinct [[Papuan language]] (or dialect) of Indonesian Papua. It is associated with the village of [[Duriankari (village)|Duriankari]] at the southern tip of the island of [[Salawati]], which is part of the [[Raja Ampat Archipelago]] and is adjacent to the [[Bird's Head Peninsula]] of the West Papuan mainland.

It was observed in the 1950s that its speakers were [[language shift|shifting]] to the [[Moi language]].{{sfn|Remijsen|2001|p=30}} Duriankari was reported in the 1980s to have had about 100 speakers,{{sfn|Berry|Berry|1987|pp=84, 92}} but by the 1990s it was said to be extinct.{{sfn|de Vries|2004|pp=1–2}} It was listed as a separate language by {{harvtxt|Voorhoeve|1975a|p=440}}, but {{harvtxt|Berry|Berry|1987|p=92}} conclude that not enough is known about it to determine whether it is a separate language or a dialect of [[Inanwatan language|Inanwatan]]. The Inanwatan language is spoken in a few villages over 150 kilometres to the east (as well as by a smaller community across the Sele Strait from Duriankari in the village of [[Seget, Indonesia|Seget]]). The Inanwatan people there regard the [[Duriankari people]] as descendants of Inanwatans who were carried away so far west by a mythical flood.{{sfn|de Vries|2004|pp=2, 96}}

A list of words in the language collected by [[J.C. Anceaux]] is available in {{harvtxt|Voorhoeve|1975b|p=104}} and {{harvtxt|Smits|Voorhoeve|1998}}.

